vet`, so I haven't checked the claim that each test fails when its guard is removed.\n- **Diff:** no `git diff` or `git status`. I read the current files, so I haven't confirmed the change is limited to the three listed files.\n- **Planned tools:** none of the `pose` tools ran, including the required `artifact-check`, which has no sealed evidence to stand in for it.\n- **Draft check:** `review draft-check` did not run.\n- **Cleanup failed:** `go clean -cache` and the `go-build*` removal did not run. I started no builds or background jobs.\n\nI'm not including a `pose-draft` block. A complete one would need me to report required tools as passed when they never ran. Please rerun the review in a session where the sandbox starts.\n\n## Defects found by reading the code\n\nNone looks like a blocker. All are unconfirmed by execution.\n\n| ID | Severity | Location | Defect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| N1 | low | `pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch.go:809` (claim at `:746`) | The directory is created after the predecessor has been claimed. If the path already exists, the interrupted run's one continuation becomes a failed, non-resumable run. Removing the directory doesn't restore `resume` on the original run, which returns the recorded failure; only a handoff or a new dispatch remains. The error at `:2052` gives no remedy. The spec's technical risk (line 73, \"fails until it is removed\") understates this. The blocking itself falls within L2 (or follows a leftover directory), but the lost resumability isn't stated there. |\n| N2 | low | `pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_run_continuity_test.go:304` | `test -f .pose/specs/alpha/spec.md \\|\\| ls >&2` can't fail the script, because a successful `ls` lets it continue to approval. The R2 test checks only that the working directory path matches, not that the checkout has content. |\n| N3 | low | `POSE.md:1045` | The resume documentation doesn't say that the checkout path is now stable per attempt, that resume needs the same `TMPDIR` (L1), or what to do when the directory already exists. I didn't check the scaffold and locale copies for equivalent text. |\n| N4 | low | `pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch.go:814-829` | If `Lstat` or `OpenRoot` fails right after the directory is created, `cleanup` is still nil. The run records cleanup as completed while the directory remains, and that now blocks later runs of the attempt. The misreport appears to predate this change; the blocking is new. It's unlikely to happen. |\n| N5 | low | `pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch.go:2049` | A process that escaped an earlier run now knows the next run's paths, including `output.txt`. This matters mainly off Linux, where `reapSealedCheckoutProcesses` does nothing. Writes inside the worktree after the baseline snapshot would be detected, but `output.txt` sits outside it. Random names previously prevented this. |\n\n## Static assessment of the main questions\n\n- **No sharing or following:** `os.Mkdir` at `:2050` refuses an existing path, including a final-component link. Attempt IDs hash a random nonce (`review_attempt_ledger.go:427-432`), and continuations are claimed exclusively before the directory is created.\n- **Cleanup:** on the normal path, `cleanupReviewResources` removes the worktree, its Git registration and then the runtime directory. This lets a resume add a worktree at the same path again. N4 is the exception I found.\n- **Runs without an attempt:** they still use `os.MkdirTemp(\"\", \"pose-review-run-*\")`.\n- **Released abandoned attempts:** a leftover directory shouldn't newly block a handoff. The release record has no brief digest (`delegation_release.go:159-169`), so the handoff digest check should already refuse it.\n- **Design (D1, R1, R3):** a stable path per attempt is a small change that addresses the stated cause without altering the sealed brief. It stops working if `TMPDIR` differs (L1). I found no reason to dispute L1 or L2 beyond the gap in N1.\n- **Round-to-round session reuse:** a later round is a new attempt, so it gets a new path. The spec's goal covers only `delegate resume`, so I haven't counted this as a defect.\n\nThe sealed evidence listed in the brief reports passes, but I didn't re-verify any of it, and I'm not marking any criterion as passed.","ttft_ms":1767,"type":"result","duration_ms":118769,"uuid":"6c605ac3-ac34-4ebf-8515-3caa9dcf8a53","ttft_stream_ms":884,"time_to_request_ms":64,"first_content_frame_ms":884,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":26,"outputTokens":15863,"cacheReadInputTokens":825714,"cacheCreationInputTokens":119942,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.7210214,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":8197,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":119942,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"credential_expires_at":"2026-12-24T05:32:18.700Z"}
